# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  theourgia_render.la — text bands, colour styles and the frame
#  compositor of the tiling window manager (Theourgia Stage 10).
#
#  The window manager draws every frame from scratch: each window is a tile
#  of scanlines (a title bar and the terminal's rows of text), and the frame
#  is the tiles laid out on a background, one XRGB8888 string that `present`
#  scans out. This module turns text into scanlines and scanlines into the
#  frame. It is the hottest code in the system, so it is built around what
#  the SECD VM actually charges for (WM_DESIGN.md "How fast the VM is").
#
#  USE. RENDER_KIT takes its builtins, then the font and the text scale:
#      RENDER_KIT(str_at)(ord)(str_to_int)(str_len)(chr)(int_to_str)(concat)
#                (add)(sub)(mul)(div)(int_eq)(lt)(band)(str_eq)(error)
#                (font)(scale)
#  where font is TF_DECODE's 768-byte table (theourgia_termfont.la) and scale
#  a positive integer (a glyph is 8*scale pixels square). It returns the
#  record (destructure it positionally, in THIS order):
#      la sel. sel(px)(run)(style)(band)(bands)(solid)(join)(compose)
#  The operations, as WM_DESIGN.md specifies them:
#    px(r)(g)(b)          one pixel: the 4 bytes B, G, R, 0 (ints 0..255)
#    run(n)(s)            n copies of s, by doubling ("" when n <= 0)
#    style(fg)(bg)        a text style; fg and bg are pixels (from px). Every
#                         glyph's 8 rows are drawn once, here, in these colours
#    band(st)(s)(cols)    Scott list of 8*scale scanlines, each cols*8*scale
#                         pixels: the first min(len s, cols) characters of s,
#                         the rest padded with the style's background. A byte
#                         outside 32..127 draws as '?'; 127 is the cursor block
#    bands(st)(cache)(texts)(cols)
#                         la k. k(list of bands)(cache'): band for each text,
#                         reusing any band in cache whose text matches (the
#                         very same strings come back). cache' holds exactly
#                         the texts used: it is the Scott list, one entry per
#                         text in texts and in that order, of la k. k(text)(band).
#                         A text repeated within one call is drawn once. Start
#                         with NIL, and pass NIL again when cols or the style
#                         changes (the cache does not record them)
#    solid(w)(h)(p)       h scanlines of w pixels of colour p: ONE string, h
#                         times in the list (nothing is copied h times)
#    join(list)           balanced concatenation of a Scott list of strings
#    compose(W)(H)(pitch)(bg)(tiles)
#                         the frame, H*pitch bytes. tiles is a Scott list of
#                         la k. k(x)(y)(w)(h)(rows), rows a Scott list of h
#                         scanlines of 4*w bytes; uncovered pixels are bg; each
#                         scanline is zero-padded from 4*W to pitch bytes
#
#  WHAT THE CONTRACT LEAVES OPEN, DEFINED HERE (the gate checks them):
#    - compose fails LOUDLY (error, exit 1) instead of drawing a wrong frame
#      when a tile breaks its contract: a tile outside the screen (x, y, w or
#      h negative, x+w > W, y+h > H), two tiles overlapping, a rows list
#      shorter or longer than h (a tile of height 0 must have no rows), or a
#      row that is not 4*w bytes; also when pitch < 4*W. H <= 0 gives "".
#    - Tiles may come in any order: compose sorts them by x, and at the same
#      x the narrower first, so a zero-width tile where another tile starts is
#      fine (one strictly inside another tile on its rows counts as overlap).
#    - band with cols <= 0 gives 8*scale empty scanlines. A line longer than
#      4095 characters halts (no screen is that wide).
#    - join of NIL is "".
#
#  HOW IT IS FAST. Measured costs on the VM (callgrind, instructions): a
#  builtin call ~850; finding a name ~13 per environment cell walked; and
#  CLOSE rescans the body of every lambda it builds, so a curried function
#  of k parameters rescans its body k-1 times per call and a Z-recursive
#  call rescans it again. concat copies 8 instructions a byte, which is
#  1.5 ns a byte. So:
#    - Kit style. Every builtin and constant is a parameter or bound once;
#      the per-character and per-row code is built from small UNARY closures
#      (applying one costs no rescan), each defined right after the names it
#      uses so they are found a few cells away. Nothing names a glyph or a
#      builtin after RENDER_KIT returns.
#    - style draws the 16 nibble strips once (4 pixels each), every glyph row
#      as two of them, and the 96 glyphs into a code tree: 8 levels over all
#      256 byte values, every node a closure  la n. lt(n)(T)(L)(R); bytes
#      outside 32..127 lie in shared constant subtrees that return the '?'
#      glyph, so there is no range test. A lookup is the unrolled walk
#      tree(c)(c)(c)(c)(c)(c)(c)(c): no Z, no counter. A leaf holds the
#      glyph's 8 scanline strips (8*scale pixels each) as a 3-level pair tree.
#    - band looks every character up ONCE, into a balanced line tree of
#      closures  la s. concat(L(s))(R(s))  (blocks of 2^k characters, built
#      level by level without Z), then runs the tree once per glyph row with a
#      selector s that picks that row from each leaf (three boolean steps).
#      The padding is ONE run of background, shared by all 8 rows. Each glyph
#      row is listed scale times: the same string, not a copy.
#    - compose first cuts the screen, from the tile geometry alone, into
#      horizontal spans in which the set of tiles does not change, and builds
#      a join PLAN: a range of rows inside one blank span is one run of the
#      blank row (doubling: 2 copies, not log2(n)); a range inside one tiled
#      span is a balanced tree shared by size (O(log n) nodes built); any other
#      range splits in half. So the H rows are joined balanced and every byte
#      is copied about log2(H)+1 times, the floor for a frame built by concat
#      (95% of compose's instructions are concat's byte copying). The tiled
#      rows come from a stream stepped by a stepper made for each span (one
#      tile, two, or more): a row costs a few small closures, not a recursion.
#      Each tile row is copied ONCE to attach what follows it on the screen:
#      the gap to the next tile, or after the last tile the tail, the pitch
#      padding and the NEXT row's leading gap (a span's first row gets its
#      leading gap in front instead), so gaps cost no extra copy of the tiles.
#  The numbers (1920x1080, scale 2) are in gate_wm_render.sh's header.
#
#  ENGINES. Pure Lingua Adamica over strings and ints, so the bytes are the
#  same on the C host and the native VM. The host is a substitution
#  interpreter, far too slow for the kit beyond tiny cases (building one
#  style there ran 11 minutes and was killed out of memory), so the gate
#  checks the VM against an independent Python model of every operation, and
#  compares host and VM on the operations that need no style (px, run, join,
#  solid, compose).
#
#  OUT OF SCOPE (honestly): no proportional or Unicode text (one byte, one
#  8x8 cell; UTF-8 is the terminal's business), no colour per character
#  within a band (a band has one style), no clipping (tiles must lie inside
#  the screen), no damage tracking: compose rebuilds the whole frame, so its
#  cost is set by the screen size, not by what changed. A faster frame is
#  possible only outside pure LA: written piece by piece to a tmpfs file
#  (open, write, read_file, all VM-only) the same 1920x1080 frame takes about
#  10 ms instead of about 100 ms; that is an I/O design decision for the
#  window manager, not part of this kit.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

export RENDER_KIT

glyph RENDER_KIT = la str_at. la ord. la str_to_int. la str_len. la chr. la int_to_str. la concat.
    la add. la sub. la mul. la div. la int_eq. la lt. la band. la str_eq. la error.
    la font. la scale.
 (la LET. la Z.
  LET(0)(la C0. LET(1)(la C1. LET(2)(la C2. LET(3)(la C3. LET(4)(la C4. LET(5)(la C5.
  LET(6)(la C6. LET(7)(la C7. LET(8)(la C8. LET(15)(la C15. LET(16)(la C16. LET(31)(la C31. LET(32)(la C32.
  LET(128)(la C128. LET(256)(la C256. LET(2048)(la C2048.
  LET(C0)(la D. LET("")(la E.
  LET(la t. la f. t)(la TT. LET(la t. la f. f)(la FF.
  LET(la n. la c. n(n))(la NIL.
  LET(la h. la t. la n. la c. c(h)(t))(la CONS.
  LET(la a. la b. la k. k(a)(b))(la PAIR.
  LET(la a. la b. la c. la k. k(a)(b)(c))(la TRIPLE.
  LET(chr(int_to_str(C0)))(la Z0.

  # ── px, run, repl, solid, join ───────────────────────────────────
  LET((la ch. la is. la cc. la Z0. la r. la g. la b.
        cc(cc(ch(is(b)))(ch(is(g))))(cc(ch(is(r)))(Z0)))
      (chr)(int_to_str)(concat)(Z0))(la px.

  # run(n)(s): halve n, double the half, add one s when n is odd.
  LET((la cc. la ie. la lt. la bd. la dv. la C0. la C1. la C2. la D. la E. la n. la s.
        Z(la self. la m.
          lt(m)(C2)
            (la _. ie(m)(C1)(la _. s)(la _. E)(D))
            (la _. (la h. (la hh. ie(bd(m)(C1))(C0)(la _. hh)(la _. cc(hh)(s))(D))(cc(h)(h)))
                   (self(dv(m)(C2))))
            (D))(n))
      (concat)(int_eq)(lt)(band)(div)(C0)(C1)(C2)(D)(E))(la run.

  # repl(n)(x)(t): n copies of x consed onto the list t.
  LET((la lt. la sb. la C1. la CONS. la D. la n. la x.
        Z(la self. la m. la t. lt(m)(C1)(la _. t)(la _. self(sb(m)(C1))(CONS(x)(t)))(D))(n))
      (lt)(sub)(C1)(CONS)(D))(la repl.

  LET(la w. la h. la p. repl(h)(run(w)(p))(NIL))(la solid.

  # LEN(l)(n): n + the length of l.
  LET((la ad. la C1. Z(la self. la l. la n. l(la _. n)(la h. la t. self(t)(ad(n)(C1)))))
      (add)(C1))(la LEN.

  # JN(n)(l) = la k. k(the first n strings of l joined, halving)(the rest).
  LET((la cc. la ie. la sb. la dv. la C1. la C2. la D. la PAIR.
        la ERR. Z(la self. la n. la l.
          ie(n)(C1)
            (la _. l(ERR)(la h. la t. PAIR(h)(t)))
            (la _. (la m. self(m)(l)(la a. la r. self(sb(n)(m))(r)(la b. la r2. PAIR(cc(a)(b))(r2))))
                   (dv(n)(C2)))
            (D)))
      (concat)(int_eq)(sub)(div)(C1)(C2)(D)(PAIR)
      (la _. error("render: join: the list is shorter than its count")))(la JN.

  LET(la l. (la n. lt(n)(C1)(la _. E)(la _. JN(n)(l)(la s. la r. s))(D))(LEN(l)(C0)))(la join.

  # ── style: the 96 glyphs drawn once, in a code tree ──────────────
  # A leaf is the pair tree ((s0,s1),(s2,s3)),((s4,s5),(s6,s7)) of a glyph's
  # 8 row strips; row r is leaf(b2)(b1)(b0), b = TT for a 0 bit of r.
  LET((la lt. la T. la L. la R. la n. lt(n)(T)(L)(R))(lt))(la NODE.

  LET(la fg. la bg.
    (la F. la B.
      (la PB.
        (la STRIP.
          (la LEAF.
            (la QL.
              (la KD.
                (la tree. la k. k(tree)(run(C8)(B)))
                (Z(la self. la lo. la sz. la d.
                   (la hi.
                     lt(lo)(C128)
                       (la _. lt(C32)(hi)
                          (la _. int_eq(sz)(C1)
                             (la _. LEAF(sub(lo)(C32)))
                             (la _. (la half. (la d1.
                                       NODE(add(lo)(half))(self(lo)(half)(d1))(self(add(lo)(half))(half)(d1)))
                                       (sub(d)(C1)))
                                    (div(sz)(C2)))
                             (D))
                          (la _. KD(d))
                          (D))
                       (la _. KD(d))
                       (D))
                   (add(lo)(sz)))(C0)(C256)(C8)))
              # KD(d): a constant subtree of depth d whose every walk ends at QL
              (Z(la self. la d. int_eq(d)(C0)(la _. QL)(la _. (la k. la n. k)(self(sub(d)(C1))))(D))))
            (LEAF(C31)))
          (la g. (la rb.
                   PAIR(PAIR(PAIR(STRIP(rb(C0)))(STRIP(rb(C1))))(PAIR(STRIP(rb(C2)))(STRIP(rb(C3)))))
                       (PAIR(PAIR(STRIP(rb(C4)))(STRIP(rb(C5))))(PAIR(STRIP(rb(C6)))(STRIP(rb(C7))))))
                 ((la base. la r. str_to_int(ord(str_at(font)(add(base)(r)))))(mul(g)(C8)))))
        # STRIP(b): the 8 pixels of row byte b, bit 0 leftmost, each scale
        # wide: the low nibble's 4 pixels, then the high nibble's, both from
        # a 16-entry tree of nibble strips drawn once per style
        ((la NT. la b. concat(NT(band(b)(C15)))(NT(div(b)(C16))))
          ((la t. la v. t(v)(v)(v)(v))
            (Z(la self. la lo. la sz.
               int_eq(sz)(C1)
                 (la _. concat(concat(PB(lo)(C1))(PB(lo)(C2)))(concat(PB(lo)(C4))(PB(lo)(C8))))
                 (la _. (la half. NODE(add(lo)(half))(self(lo)(half))(self(add(lo)(half))(half)))
                        (div(sz)(C2)))
                 (D))(C0)(C16)))))
      (la b. la m. int_eq(band(b)(m))(C0)(B)(F)))
    (run(scale)(fg))(run(scale)(bg)))(la style.

  # ── band: the line tree, run once per glyph row ──────────────────
  LET((la cc. la L. la R. la s. cc(L(s))(R(s)))(concat))(la N.
  LET((la cc. la P. la Q. la s. cc(s(P))(s(Q)))(concat))(la N1.
  LET(la P. la s. s(P))(la W1.
  LET(la p. la s. p)(la PADL.
  LET((la T. la F. la P. P(T)(T)(T))(TT)(FF))(la S0.
  LET((la T. la F. la P. P(T)(T)(F))(TT)(FF))(la S1.
  LET((la T. la F. la P. P(T)(F)(T))(TT)(FF))(la S2.
  LET((la T. la F. la P. P(T)(F)(F))(TT)(FF))(la S3.
  LET((la T. la F. la P. P(F)(T)(T))(TT)(FF))(la S4.
  LET((la T. la F. la P. P(F)(T)(F))(TT)(FF))(la S5.
  LET((la T. la F. la P. P(F)(F)(T))(TT)(FF))(la S6.
  LET((la T. la F. la P. P(F)(F)(F))(TT)(FF))(la S7.
  # MLF(tree)(s)(i): the leaf of character i of s
  LET((la sa. la od. la si. la tree. la s. la i.
        (la c. tree(c)(c)(c)(c)(c)(c)(c)(c))(si(od(sa(s)(i)))))
      (str_at)(ord)(str_to_int))(la MLF.
  # blocks of 1, 2, then 2w from two blocks of w
  LET((la W1. la lf. la i. W1(lf(i)))(W1))(la MB0.
  LET((la ad. la C1. la N1. la lf. la i. N1(lf(i))(lf(ad(i)(C1))))(add)(C1)(N1))(la MB1.
  LET((la ad. la N. la f. la w. la i. N(f(i))(f(ad(i)(w))))(add)(N))(la LV.
  # LEVELS(lf): Scott list of PAIR(block)(width), widest (2048) first
  LET((la ie. la ad. la CONS. la PAIR. la NIL. la C1. la C2. la CMAX. la D. la LV. la MB0. la MB1. la lf.
        Z(la self. la b. la w. la acc.
           ie(w)(CMAX)
             (la _. CONS(PAIR(b)(w))(acc))
             (la _. self(LV(b)(w))(ad(w)(w))(CONS(PAIR(b)(w))(acc)))
             (D))(MB1(lf))(C2)(CONS(PAIR(MB0(lf))(C1))(NIL)))
      (int_eq)(add)(CONS)(PAIR)(NIL)(C1)(C2)(C2048)(D)(LV)(MB0)(MB1))(la LEVELS.
  # DEC(hp)(pad)(levels)(pos)(rem): the line tree of characters [pos, pos+rem),
  # then the padding leaf when hp; the binary digits of rem pick the blocks
  LET((la ie. la lt. la ad. la sb. la C0. la D. la N. la PADL.
        la ERR. la hp. la pad.
        Z(la self. la lv. la pos. la rem.
           lv(ERR)(la e. la more. e(la B. la w.
             lt(rem)(w)
               (la _. self(more)(pos)(rem))
               (la _. (la r2. (la blk.
                         ie(r2)(C0)
                           (la _. hp(la _. N(blk)(PADL(pad)))(la _. blk)(D))
                           (la _. N(blk)(self(more)(ad(pos)(w))(r2)))
                           (D))
                       (B(pos)))
                      (sb(rem)(w)))
               (D)))))
      (int_eq)(lt)(add)(sub)(C0)(D)(N)(PADL)
      (la _. error("render: band: the line is longer than 4095 characters")))(la DEC.
  # RP(x)(t): x listed scale times in front of t
  LET(repl(scale))(la RP.

  LET((la si. la sl. la ie. la lt. la sb. la C0. la D. la NIL. la run. la RP. la DEC. la LEVELS. la MLF.
       la S0. la S1. la S2. la S3. la S4. la S5. la S6. la S7.
       la st. la s. la cols.
       st(la tree. la bgs.
         (la len. (la n. (la pad.
            ie(n)(C0)
              (la _. RP(pad)(RP(pad)(RP(pad)(RP(pad)(RP(pad)(RP(pad)(RP(pad)(RP(pad)(NIL)))))))))
              (la _. (la line.
                       RP(line(S0))(RP(line(S1))(RP(line(S2))(RP(line(S3))(
                       RP(line(S4))(RP(line(S5))(RP(line(S6))(RP(line(S7))(NIL)))))))))
                     (DEC(lt(n)(cols))(pad)(LEVELS(MLF(tree)(s)))(C0)(n)))
              (D))
           (run(sb(cols)(n))(bgs)))
           (lt(len)(cols)(len)(lt(cols)(C0)(C0)(cols))))
           (si(sl(s)))))
      (str_to_int)(str_len)(int_eq)(lt)(sub)(C0)(D)(NIL)(run)(RP)(DEC)(LEVELS)(MLF)
      (S0)(S1)(S2)(S3)(S4)(S5)(S6)(S7))(la tband.

  # ── bands: one band per text, reusing a cache ────────────────────
  # FIND(t)(l): la n. la s. n(D) when no entry of l has text t, else
  # s(that entry's band)(the entries after it).
  LET((la seq. la D. la t.
        Z(la self. la l.
          l(la _. la n. la s. n(D))
           (la h. la tl. h(la ht. la hb. seq(t)(ht)(la _. la n. la s. s(hb)(tl))(la _. self(tl))(D)))))
      (str_eq)(D))(la FIND.

  LET(la st. la cache. la texts. la cols.
    (la BAND.
      Z(la self. la ts. la ptr. la fresh.
        ts(la _. PAIR(NIL)(NIL))
          (la t. la rest.
             (FIND(t)(ptr)
                (la _. FIND(t)(cache)
                   (la _. FIND(t)(fresh)
                      (la _. (la b. TRIPLE(b)(ptr)(CONS(PAIR(t)(b))(fresh)))(BAND(t)))
                      (la b. la r. TRIPLE(b)(ptr)(fresh)))
                   (la b. la r. TRIPLE(b)(r)(fresh)))
                (la b. la r. TRIPLE(b)(r)(fresh)))
             (la b. la p2. la f2.
                self(rest)(p2)(f2)(la bs. la cs. PAIR(CONS(b)(bs))(CONS(PAIR(t)(b))(cs))))))
        (texts)(cache)(NIL))
    (la t. tband(st)(t)(cols)))(la bands.

  # ── compose ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The frame is joined by a PLAN built from the tile geometry alone (no row
  # is looked at to build it): rows that lie in one blank span are one run of
  # the blank row; a range inside one tiled span is a shared balanced tree
  # TP(n) of row pulls; any other range splits in half. The tiled rows come
  # from a STREAM, a closure st with st(K) = K(next row)(st'). In a span the
  # stream steps the active tiles' row lists with a stepper made for that span
  # (one tile, two, or more); each tile row goes through its unit function,
  # which checks it is 4*w bytes and puts behind it what follows it on the
  # screen: the gap to the next tile, or after the last tile the tail, the
  # pitch padding AND the next row's leading gap. So every tile row is copied
  # once to attach its gaps (not once per gap); the span's first row gets its
  # leading gap in front instead, and its last row ends at the tail.
  LET((la cc. la L. la R. la st. la K. L(st)(la a. la s1. R(s1)(la b. la s2. K(cc(a)(b))(s2))))
      (concat))(la PNODE.
  LET(la st. st)(la PROW.
  # TPP(n) = PAIR(TP(n))(TP(n+1)), TP(n) the plan of n tiled rows (first half
  # floor(n/2)); O(log n) nodes are built, the rest are shared
  LET((la ie. la bd. la dv. la C0. la C1. la C2. la D. la PAIR. la PNODE. la PROW.
        Z(la self. la n.
          ie(n)(C1)
            (la _. PAIR(PROW)(PNODE(PROW)(PROW)))
            (la _. self(dv(n)(C2))(la a. la b.
                     ie(bd(n)(C1))(C0)
                       (la _. PAIR(PNODE(a)(a))(PNODE(a)(b)))
                       (la _. PAIR(PNODE(a)(b))(PNODE(b)(b)))
                       (D)))
            (D)))
      (int_eq)(band)(div)(C0)(C1)(C2)(D)(PAIR)(PNODE)(PROW))(la TPP.

  # steppers: step(s)(last)(K) = K(the next row)(s'): s the span's tile row
  # lists; last is TRUE on the span's last row, where the last tile's unit
  # function differs (no next row's gap behind it)
  LET(la _. error("render: compose: a tile has fewer rows than its height"))(la ERRS.
  LET((la ES. la Um. la Ul. la l. la last. la K. l(ES)(la x. la xt. K(last(Ul)(Um)(x))(xt)))
      (ERRS))(la STEP1.
  LET((la cc. la PAIR. la ES. la U1. la Um. la Ul. la s. la last. la K.
        s(la l1. la l2. l1(ES)(la x. la xt. l2(ES)(la y. la yt. K(cc(U1(x))(last(Ul)(Um)(y)))(PAIR(xt)(yt))))))
      (concat)(PAIR)(ERRS))(la STEP2.
  # POPN(units)(lists)(K) = K(the units of the next row)(the advanced lists)
  LET((la CONS. la NIL. la ES.
        Z(la self. la us. la ls. la K.
          us(la _. K(NIL)(NIL))
            (la u. la ur. ls(ES)(la l. la lr. l(ES)(la x. la xt.
               self(ur)(lr)(la a. la b. K(CONS(u(x))(a))(CONS(xt)(b))))))))
      (CONS)(NIL)(ERRS))(la POPN.
  LET((la POPN. la JN. la k. la USm. la USl. la ls. la last. la K.
        POPN(last(USl)(USm))(ls)(la us. la ls2. K(JN(k)(us)(la a. la b. a))(ls2)))
      (POPN)(JN))(la STEPN.
  # STEPFOR(units of the middle rows)(units of the last row)(K) =
  # K(step)(pack)(unpack): the span's stepper, and the conversions between a
  # list of row lists and the stepper's state
  LET((la CONS. la NIL. la PAIR. la LEN. la C0. la STEP1. la STEP2. la STEPN.
        la ERR. la um. la ul. la K.
        um(ERR)(la u1. la r1. ul(ERR)(la l1. la q1.
          r1(la _. K(STEP1(u1)(l1))(la ls. ls(ERR)(la a. la r. a))(la s. CONS(s)(NIL)))
            (la u2. la r2. q1(ERR)(la l2. la q2.
              r2(la _. K(STEP2(u1)(u2)(l2))
                       (la ls. ls(ERR)(la a. la r. r(ERR)(la b. la r3. PAIR(a)(b))))
                       (la s. s(la a. la b. CONS(a)(CONS(b)(NIL)))))
